WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.760
In the first film, internalized receptors reached the early endosome with their

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.880
tails still facing the cytosol.

3
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:15.400
Can an active receptor keep signaling from there, and what finally shuts it off?

4
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.300
Surprisingly, entering the cell does not by itself silence an active receptor.

5
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.400
Inside the cell sounds isolated, yet the way the membrane faces says otherwise.

6
00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:34.400
On the outer endosome membrane, an active receptor's tail still touches cytosolic

7
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.960
partner proteins. Some receptors keep sending the same signal.

8
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.800
Others even switch partners once they leave the plasma membrane.

9
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:48.680
Toll-like receptor 4 shows that location switch clearly.

10
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.720
At the plasma membrane, it binds a cytosolic adaptor called MyD88.

11
00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:02.020
After endocytosis into an endosome, it binds a second adaptor, called TRIF, which

12
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.780
switches on interferon genes.

13
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.070
So how does a cell silence an endosomal receptor?

14
00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:14.830
A receptor leaves the recycling route when an enzyme attaches a small tag called

15
00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:21.570
ubiquitin. A protein complex on the endosome, called ESCRT, reads ubiquitin and

16
00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.730
gathers tagged receptors into one patch.

17
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.353
Watch which way ESCRT bends the membrane.

18
00:01:28.793 --> 00:01:32.733
Coated pits pulled membrane into the cytosol, leaving tails exposed.

19
00:01:34.233 --> 00:01:38.993
ESCRT pushes the tagged patch the opposite way, inward into the endosome fluid.

20
00:01:40.273 --> 00:01:44.853
It pinches off as a small vesicle inside a multivesicular endosome.

21
00:01:45.817 --> 00:01:48.557
Check which way the receptor tail faces now.

22
00:01:49.737 --> 00:01:55.237
The receptor sits sealed inside that internal vesicle before any lysosome arrives.

23
00:01:56.597 --> 00:02:01.217
Does its signal stop right now, or only after digestive enzymes break it down?

24
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.440
Its signal stops right now, because membrane now encloses the cytoplasmic tail

25
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:18.960
completely. Cytosolic partners can no longer reach it, so signaling ends before

26
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.340
digestion even begins.

27
00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.460
That step also places the whole receptor inside for breakdown.

28
00:02:26.683 --> 00:02:33.003
Meanwhile, cyan Rab5 leaves as purple Rab7 arrives, a swap called Rab conversion.

29
00:02:34.343 --> 00:02:39.543
The late endosome grows more acid, then fuses with a lysosome near pH 5.

30
00:02:40.463 --> 00:02:48.023
Each pH unit down means 10× times more protons: 7 to 6 is 10×, -fold, 7 to 5 100×.

31
00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:52.927
Break that sorting tag, and signaling runs too long.

32
00:02:54.207 --> 00:03:00.227
In some lung cancers, the receptor MET loses a short segment holding the docking

33
00:03:00.227 --> 00:03:02.227
site for its ubiquitin-adding enzyme.

34
00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:07.667
Left poorly tagged, MET slips past ESCRT and keeps signaling.

35
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.840
Endocytosis alone does not turn an active receptor off.

36
00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.480
Mild acid can silence a receptor earlier if its ligand lets go.

37
00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:25.280
Otherwise, the way the tail faces sets the cutoff: active receptors can signal from

38
00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.860
endosomes until sealed inside internal vesicles.
